WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:02.360
Speaker 1: It's great to welcome to the program
today.

00:00:02.360 --> 00:00:08.130
Democratic Congressman Jamie Raskin, who represents
Maryland's eighth Congressional District,

00:00:08.130 --> 00:00:14.720
was the lead House manager in the second impeachment
trial of former President Donald Trump.

00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:17.170
Congressman, it's so great to have you on.

00:00:17.170 --> 00:00:20.440
Speaker 5: I'm delighted to be with you, David.

00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:21.440
Thank you for having me.

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:26.410
Speaker 1: So, okay, to start with, we're
going to have starting with in primetime on

00:00:26.410 --> 00:00:32.680
June 9th, these public hearings related to
the January six committee.

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:35.940
And there are sort of two opposing forces
here.

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:40.260
There's one side, yourself included, who have
been saying what's going to be revealed during

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:43.430
those hearings is going to be mind blowing.

00:00:43.430 --> 00:00:46.730
That's my term, not necessarily a term that
you have used.

00:00:46.730 --> 00:00:50.170
The other side is this feeling that nothing's
really going to happen.

00:00:50.170 --> 00:00:53.940
Nobody's ultimately going to be held accountable
for what happened.

00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:58.580
What can you say to those in my audience who
are in that latter group who are concerned

00:00:58.580 --> 00:01:00.540
about lack of accountability?

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:10.899
Speaker 5: Well, this is all part of the process
of making the events of January six visible,

00:01:10.899 --> 00:01:15.330
comprehensible to people and making them the
basis of real accountability.

00:01:15.330 --> 00:01:21.130
Now, some of that accountability is legal
accountability, and that's really within the

00:01:21.130 --> 00:01:26.369
realm of the Department of Justice, which
has brought more than 800 prosecutions in

00:01:26.369 --> 00:01:31.229
the most sweeping investigation in the history
of the DOJ.

00:01:31.229 --> 00:01:34.810
Obviously, we're all on the edge of our seats
to see if they're going to work their way

00:01:34.810 --> 00:01:41.599
all the way up to the top, or whether Trump,
as a kind of mob boss, is able to insulate

00:01:41.599 --> 00:01:48.409
himself with several layers of buffer between
him and the criminal actions and the legal

00:01:48.409 --> 00:01:50.229
consequences of those actions.

00:01:50.229 --> 00:01:55.549
But I do think that the investigation is proceeding
apace and they're working their way up the

00:01:55.549 --> 00:02:01.559
way that a lot of organized crime investigations
work their way up the ladder.

00:02:01.559 --> 00:02:05.200
But then there's there's public and political
accountability.

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:08.530
And that's really what the January 6th Select
Committee is all about.

00:02:08.530 --> 00:02:14.980
I mean, under House Resolution 503, our job
is to report to Congress and to the American

00:02:14.980 --> 00:02:20.780
people about what happened on January 6th
and what were the causes behind it, and then

00:02:20.780 --> 00:02:25.310
to make recommendations about how to fortify
our institutions going forward.

00:02:25.310 --> 00:02:32.349
Speaker 1: Do you expect some of your colleagues
in the House may have legal liability?

00:02:32.349 --> 00:02:36.680
Speaker 5: Well.

00:02:36.680 --> 00:02:44.069
You know, first of all, again, that's not
really our role.

00:02:44.069 --> 00:02:45.750
In other words, understood.

00:02:45.750 --> 00:02:55.220
We are now we're turning over evidence of
people who are in of Congress because they

00:02:55.220 --> 00:02:57.030
refuse to participate.

00:02:57.030 --> 00:03:06.150
For example, Dan Scavino and Peter Navarro,
you know, they never gave a single documents

00:03:06.150 --> 00:03:07.150
to the committee.

00:03:07.150 --> 00:03:08.719
They never spent one minute testifying.

00:03:08.719 --> 00:03:11.689
They just blew us off.

00:03:11.689 --> 00:03:14.300
Mark Meadows is kind of one foot in and one
foot out.

00:03:14.300 --> 00:03:21.069
Doing the hokey pokey gave us some stuff and
then Trump got mad, withdrew, you know, Steve

00:03:21.069 --> 00:03:22.069
Bannon's stuff.

00:03:22.069 --> 00:03:25.260
We've turned all of that information over
to the Department of Justice.

00:03:25.260 --> 00:03:30.459
We voted to hold them in contempt, and now
it's up to the executive branch and the DOJ

00:03:30.459 --> 00:03:32.889
to enforce that.

00:03:32.889 --> 00:03:38.790
In terms of evidence of other crimes, we are
in the process of laying them out there.

00:03:38.790 --> 00:03:46.790
And, you know, in the John Eastman litigation,
when Judge Carter was dealing with Eastman's

00:03:46.790 --> 00:03:53.150
claim that he was somehow covered by attorney
client privilege, the judge asked the question,

00:03:53.150 --> 00:03:54.770
well, was there an attorney client privilege?

00:03:54.770 --> 00:03:56.140
Was it waived?

00:03:56.140 --> 00:03:57.959
And what did it.

00:03:57.959 --> 00:04:02.700
Does it even apply if it existed because of
the crime fraud exception?

00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:05.749
And we said there was no attorney client privilege.

00:04:05.749 --> 00:04:07.779
If there was, it was waived.

00:04:07.779 --> 00:04:14.680
And, you know, in any event, it doesn't apply
because all of these crimes were committed

00:04:14.680 --> 00:04:16.400
and we set them forth.

00:04:16.400 --> 00:04:19.079
And Judge Carter basically adopted what we
had to say.

00:04:19.079 --> 00:04:25.580
So we've not been shy about setting forth
statutory offenses.

00:04:25.580 --> 00:04:37.280
But yet, as much as we all would like to see
Donald Trump and others in his inner echelon

00:04:37.280 --> 00:04:44.710
held legally accountable, far more important
is whether or not we can solidify our democratic

00:04:44.710 --> 00:04:50.180
institutions and keep the engines of democracy
turning.

00:04:50.180 --> 00:04:56.500
I mean, that's just so much more important
than, you know, how Donald Trump spends the

00:04:56.500 --> 00:05:00.610
remaining years of his life, whether it's
behind bars or somewhere else.

00:05:00.610 --> 00:05:05.449
I care a lot more about whether or not we
are going to be able to bequeath the democracy,

00:05:05.449 --> 00:05:12.009
a working, functional democracy to our children
and our grandchildren, because the autocrats,

00:05:12.009 --> 00:05:16.940
the dictators, the despots are on the march
all over the world.

00:05:16.940 --> 00:05:27.060
And from Moscow to Mar a Lago, they're working
to undermine democratic process and to drain

00:05:27.060 --> 00:05:29.010
life from our institutions.

00:05:29.010 --> 00:05:32.610
And we've got to make sure we've got something
to give our kids.

00:05:32.610 --> 00:05:36.690
Speaker 1: Yeah, to that end, I mean, it seems
very clear that at least to some degree, some

00:05:36.690 --> 00:05:40.759
of the people who are trying to avoid coming
before your committee are trying to run out

00:05:40.759 --> 00:05:44.700
the clock, knowing that if Republicans take
the House in November and are sworn in in

00:05:44.700 --> 00:05:47.819
January, this committee is is essentially
over.

00:05:47.819 --> 00:05:53.720
Is your plan to be done long before that in
order to prevent an interruption before you're

00:05:53.720 --> 00:06:02.099
done with your work as a result of 
the election?

00:06:02.099 --> 00:06:06.690
Speaker 5: You're able to have our hearings
begin tonight.

00:06:06.690 --> 00:06:13.139
There will be six hearings at least that take
place in the month of June.

00:06:13.139 --> 00:06:21.240
There will be perhaps another hearing or two
in the future to deliver the recommendations

00:06:21.240 --> 00:06:22.240
of the committee.

00:06:22.240 --> 00:06:28.229
And there will be a report, and we're hoping
to have all of that done early in the fall.

00:06:28.229 --> 00:06:33.190
You know, it's not just our committee that
ends with this Congress.

00:06:33.190 --> 00:06:40.040
Everything ends with this Congress because
House of Representatives, unlike the Senate,

00:06:40.040 --> 00:06:43.259
is a new a newly constituted body every two
years.

00:06:43.259 --> 00:06:47.169
You know, we come to the end of the Congress
and then there's a new Congress that forms

00:06:47.169 --> 00:06:51.789
the Senate because of the overlapping nature
of their terms, is a continuing body.

00:06:51.789 --> 00:06:57.720
They only lose one third of their members
or one third of their members are up for reelection.

00:06:57.720 --> 00:07:03.300
Whereas our entire body is up for reelection
and we adopt new rules and outs and so on.

00:07:03.300 --> 00:07:07.110
So it's not any kind of strategic political
calculus.

00:07:07.110 --> 00:07:11.689
It's just that we've got to get this done
before this session of Congress ends.

00:07:11.689 --> 00:07:12.699
Speaker 1: Okay.

00:07:12.699 --> 00:07:17.939
When it comes to gun safety, there have been
some of your colleagues who have said something

00:07:17.939 --> 00:07:19.900
feels different this time.

00:07:19.900 --> 00:07:26.229
We've seen a couple of Republicans, including
Adam Kinzinger and Congressman Jacobs, who

00:07:26.229 --> 00:07:30.900
have said, I actually have a new position
when it comes to either an assault weapons

00:07:30.900 --> 00:07:33.020
ban or a AR 15.

00:07:33.020 --> 00:07:36.150
There's something that that might be a little
bit different this time.

00:07:36.150 --> 00:07:40.350
But we've seen this cycle for a long time,
which is there's a mass shooting.

00:07:40.350 --> 00:07:42.990
Everybody says we must do something.

00:07:42.990 --> 00:07:44.430
Republicans come in and say, oh, yeah, sure.

00:07:44.430 --> 00:07:47.939
But that can include actually dealing with
gun safety.

00:07:47.939 --> 00:07:51.819
And then there's no major shooting in the
news for a couple of weeks and nothing happens

00:07:51.819 --> 00:07:55.080
and it's rinse repeat for year after year
after year.

00:07:55.080 --> 00:07:59.240
Is your genuine opinion that this is going
to be that same cycle or that maybe something

00:07:59.240 --> 00:08:00.879
will be different this time?

00:08:00.879 --> 00:08:09.090
Speaker 5: Well, you know, my my colleagues
senior to me believe it is going to be the

00:08:09.090 --> 00:08:16.699
exact same thing it's been before, because
the NRA exerts such a stranglehold over the

00:08:16.699 --> 00:08:21.979
GOP and in the final analysis, they will refuse
to move.

00:08:21.979 --> 00:08:30.729
On the other hand, you know, I'm of the mind
that the Republicans think that they are in

00:08:30.729 --> 00:08:37.460
a place where they can actually seize control
of the House and the Senate and they can win

00:08:37.460 --> 00:08:38.460
these elections.

00:08:38.460 --> 00:08:45.960
You know, they've obviously been vilifying
and demonizing Joe Biden, and yet they're

00:08:45.960 --> 00:08:51.460
starting to realize that the public is overwhelmingly
against them on all the big issues.

00:08:51.460 --> 00:08:54.081
I mean, you know, they are the dog.

00:08:54.081 --> 00:08:58.860
They caught the car when it comes to destroying
Rove versus Wade and the constitutional right

00:08:58.860 --> 00:08:59.930
to privacy.

00:08:59.930 --> 00:09:08.670
So that creates explosive political conditions
where the women of America can turn en masse

00:09:08.670 --> 00:09:16.020
against the GOP and what they've done to destroy
women's reproductive freedom.

00:09:16.020 --> 00:09:23.780
Similarly, the the gun violence is so out
of control and so dangerous and frightening

00:09:23.780 --> 00:09:32.900
to people that there could be they could experience
this terrible break with young people in America

00:09:32.900 --> 00:09:35.090
who just say enough is enough.

00:09:35.090 --> 00:09:44.100
And the young people who really rocked American
politics in Parkland, Florida, with the March

00:09:44.100 --> 00:09:49.620
for Our Lives are organizing another march.

00:09:49.620 --> 00:09:54.370
And people are saying that hundreds of thousands
or maybe even a million people could come

00:09:54.370 --> 00:09:55.380
to Washington.

00:09:55.380 --> 00:10:02.090
So I think that the GOP may have the sense
that their big chance to do what they really

00:10:02.090 --> 00:10:08.910
care about most, which is taking power, could
all be squandered because of the recklessness

00:10:08.910 --> 00:10:10.040
of their policies.

00:10:10.040 --> 00:10:16.250
And so that would argue that they might try
to do something so that they're not so clearly

00:10:16.250 --> 00:10:18.610
the party that is tagged with all these massacres.

00:10:18.610 --> 00:10:24.240
One of my constituents wrote me the other
day with the idea that the massacres should

00:10:24.240 --> 00:10:30.930
be named after the Republican members of Congress
and the senators whose districts and states

00:10:30.930 --> 00:10:32.010
they represent.

00:10:32.010 --> 00:10:37.630
When those are the members who are doing nothing
to get us a universal, violent criminal background

00:10:37.630 --> 00:10:45.470
check, doing nothing to close the, you know,
the gun show loophole, the Internet loophole,

00:10:45.470 --> 00:10:51.770
the private sale loophole, doing nothing to
take weapons of war out of the hands of 18

00:10:51.770 --> 00:10:59.300
year olds and, you know, essentially reproduce
the slaughter, which puts America into a class

00:10:59.300 --> 00:11:00.530
completely of its own.

00:11:00.530 --> 00:11:11.010
I mean, you know, we're suffering gun casualties
20 times, 25, 50 times higher than other industrialized

00:11:11.010 --> 00:11:12.010
countries.

00:11:12.010 --> 00:11:13.160
And it's all because.

00:11:13.160 --> 00:11:21.500
Speaker 1: You know, on that note, many of
my liberal friends who are for all sorts of

00:11:21.500 --> 00:11:26.960
different gun safety regulations, which I
know you support and which I support, also

00:11:26.960 --> 00:11:32.940
are of the mindset that they don't love the
idea of only the right wingers having the

00:11:32.940 --> 00:11:38.680
guns and they are increasingly seeking firearms
themselves and obtaining them almost like

00:11:38.680 --> 00:11:41.270
a race to the bottom of sorts, if you will.

00:11:41.270 --> 00:11:43.670
Do you do you sympathize with that fear?

00:11:43.670 --> 00:11:45.290
Does that does that make sense to you?

00:11:45.290 --> 00:11:46.590
To some degree.

00:11:46.590 --> 00:11:54.750
Speaker 5: Well, I mean, the Supreme Court,
of course, in Heller versus District of Columbia,

00:11:54.750 --> 00:12:01.230
said that the core of the individual right
under the Second Amendment is the right of

00:12:01.230 --> 00:12:03.260
self-defense in your home.

00:12:03.260 --> 00:12:06.520
And people have that right.

00:12:06.520 --> 00:12:11.480
That's deeply anchored in American culture
and law.

00:12:11.480 --> 00:12:21.550
And so if there are people who are fearful
for that reason and they feel that firearms

00:12:21.550 --> 00:12:29.310
will keep them safe, you know, they have a
right to exercise their Second Amendment rights

00:12:29.310 --> 00:12:30.310
in that way.

00:12:30.310 --> 00:12:34.550
Of course, the liberals in the Supreme Court
had advanced the traditional view, which is

00:12:34.550 --> 00:12:41.660
that the Second Amendment is about the right
to possess guns in connection with militia

00:12:41.660 --> 00:12:42.660
service.

00:12:42.660 --> 00:12:47.390
In other words, you read the prefatory clause
without a regulated militia being necessary

00:12:47.390 --> 00:12:53.470
to the survival of the free state comma in
conjunction with the operative clause, the

00:12:53.470 --> 00:12:58.350
right of the people to keep and bear arms
so that in any event, that the conservative

00:12:58.350 --> 00:12:59.450
majority won on that.

00:12:59.450 --> 00:13:07.790
But even Justice Scalia was emphatic that
reasonable gun regulation is totally within

00:13:07.790 --> 00:13:14.530
the contemplation of the Second Amendment,
and you're going to have to sell assault weapons

00:13:14.530 --> 00:13:21.240
to anybody, and you don't have to allow kids
to get weapons and you don't have to allow

00:13:21.240 --> 00:13:23.850
people to take weapons into public places.

00:13:23.850 --> 00:13:31.080
You know, I hope we have not arrived at civil
war style conditions, but I you know, I think

00:13:31.080 --> 00:13:36.010
it's perfectly healthy for people across the
political spectrum to exercise their Second

00:13:36.010 --> 00:13:37.010
Amendment rights.

00:13:37.010 --> 00:13:41.490
But it's also perfectly healthy for us to
understand what Second Amendment rights really

00:13:41.490 --> 00:13:47.610
are right, and what the authority is to guarantee
public safety, because that's essential to

00:13:47.610 --> 00:13:48.650
the social contract.

00:13:48.650 --> 00:13:53.360
And the whole reason we get into a social
contract, if you read Locke and Hobbs and

00:13:53.360 --> 00:14:00.910
Rousseau, is that we sort of surrendered the
rights of violent self-help in exchange for

00:14:00.910 --> 00:14:03.670
security in a civil society.

00:14:03.670 --> 00:14:08.980
But the Republicans who refused to do nothing
essentially, you know, give us the worst of

00:14:08.980 --> 00:14:10.090
both worlds.

00:14:10.090 --> 00:14:15.010
They compel kids to go to school under the
truancy laws, under the mandatory attendance

00:14:15.010 --> 00:14:20.660
laws, but then they leave them as sitting
ducks because they won't do anything to keep

00:14:20.660 --> 00:14:24.160
them safe from mass slaughter with assault
weapons.

00:14:24.160 --> 00:14:28.290
Speaker 1: Congressman, last thing I want
to ask you about, and I know our time is not

00:14:28.290 --> 00:14:29.290
unlimited.

00:14:29.290 --> 00:14:32.680
I am not an advocate of a primary challenge
to Joe Biden.

00:14:32.680 --> 00:14:37.350
I think if Joe Biden decides to run for reelection,
that he should be the Democratic nominee from

00:14:37.350 --> 00:14:39.220
a strategic point of view.

00:14:39.220 --> 00:14:43.630
That being said, when people say to me, David,
is there anyone you think would be interesting

00:14:43.630 --> 00:14:45.950
to see as the Democratic nominee?

00:14:45.950 --> 00:14:49.540
To be totally honest, I think of you I think
of Jon Ossoff.

00:14:49.540 --> 00:14:52.140
I think Jon Ossoff would be very interesting.

00:14:52.140 --> 00:14:57.360
I know that it would be a crazy thing to ask
you if it's something you're think about thinking

00:14:57.360 --> 00:14:58.360
about.

00:14:58.360 --> 00:14:59.360
You would never answer it.

00:14:59.360 --> 00:15:00.360
It would be inappropriate.

00:15:00.360 --> 00:15:01.360
It would be a crazy thing to ask.

00:15:01.360 --> 00:15:08.620
But my more specific question is, one of the
things I do think is I think that unfortunately,

00:15:08.620 --> 00:15:15.420
because of anti-Semitism on both the right
and some on the left, I think Jewish presidential

00:15:15.420 --> 00:15:18.970
candidates would still struggle because of
anti-Semitism.

00:15:18.970 --> 00:15:21.830
What's your opinion about that issue in America
right now?

00:15:21.830 --> 00:15:23.540
Speaker 5: Huh?

00:15:23.540 --> 00:15:27.070
I hadn't really thought that through, David.

00:15:27.070 --> 00:15:28.880
Well, let me see.

00:15:28.880 --> 00:15:33.610
And I wonder if you're asking me this, because
I happen to be in New Hampshire campaigning

00:15:33.610 --> 00:15:39.390
for my friends Chris Pappas and Annie Kuster
and for Senator Maggie Hassan.

00:15:39.390 --> 00:15:46.840
But, you know, look, I think it's really important
for the young people of America to understand

00:15:46.840 --> 00:15:51.940
and the kids of America to understand, despite
what they might see on the Internet and despite

00:15:51.940 --> 00:15:56.850
what they might hear from some people in public
office.

00:15:56.850 --> 00:15:58.860
We are not a racist country.

00:15:58.860 --> 00:16:01.430
We are not an anti-Semitic country.

00:16:01.430 --> 00:16:04.900
We are not a sexist, misogynistic country.

00:16:04.900 --> 00:16:08.390
We are not an immigrant bashing country.

00:16:08.390 --> 00:16:14.930
And I believe so much in, you know, what Tom
Paine saw when he got to America.

00:16:14.930 --> 00:16:22.110
I mean, he said this country could become
an asylum for humanity, not an insane asylum,

00:16:22.110 --> 00:16:28.210
mind you, but it could become a place of refuge
for people fleeing from every form of political,

00:16:28.210 --> 00:16:33.190
religious, economic repression and discrimination
on Earth.

00:16:33.190 --> 00:16:38.960
And I think despite what we've lived with
through Donald Trump and the Proud Boys and

00:16:38.960 --> 00:16:44.480
the Oath Keepers and the militia groups and
the three percenters, and despite all of the

00:16:44.480 --> 00:16:50.750
divisiveness and the polarization, I still
believe we are the world's greatest multiracial,

00:16:50.750 --> 00:16:54.930
multi-religious, multi ethnic, constitutional
democracy.

00:16:54.930 --> 00:17:01.230
And the sky's the limit for what we can make
happen for our people once we fight off all

00:17:01.230 --> 00:17:06.850
of these authoritarian, racist attacks on
our country.

00:17:06.850 --> 00:17:13.949
So, you know, there there would be a lot of
reasons that I wouldn't run for president,

00:17:13.949 --> 00:17:16.870
but being Jewish would not be one of them.

00:17:16.870 --> 00:17:21.299
Speaker 1: Congressman Jamie Raskin, I really
appreciate your time.

00:17:21.299 --> 00:17:24.480
And we're going to be watching next week as
the hearings start very closely.

00:17:24.480 --> 00:17:25.480
Thank you so much.

00:17:25.480 --> 00:17:27.869
Speaker 5: Thanks so much for having me, David

